{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"customable-desirable-yucker","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Capability call sites are cast, not guarded — a non-capable transport fails silently\n\n## The gap\n\nTicket `cc77-8120-bcc6-47e8` annotated the core reconciler's `client`/`transport` parameters with\nthe `TicketTransport` port. Four call sites then reported `[attr-defined]` because they reach for\nOPT-IN capability members that the base port deliberately does not declare:\n\n| site | member |\n|---|---|\n| `dispatch_one.py:465` (`create_one`) | `add_comment` |\n| `dispatch_one.py:667` (`_update_one_scalar_update`) | `add_comment` |\n| `dispatch_one.py:752` (`_update_one_dispatch_links`) | `set_relationship` |\n| `dispatch_apply_phases.py:165` (`_update_one_dispatch_comments`) | `add_comment` |\n\nEach was correctly resolved with `cast(\"SupportsComments\", client)` / `cast(\"SupportsLinks\", client)`\n— correct, because widening to `Any` would have destroyed the enforcement cc77 exists to add.\n\n**But a `cast` is a static assertion with NO runtime effect.** None of the four sites checks that the\ntransport actually HAS the member. Compare `fetcher.py:553` and `fetcher.py:590`, which gate the same\nclass of optional capability on `hasattr` before calling. The reconciler is therefore inconsistent\nwith itself: one subsystem guards, the other asserts.\n\n## Why it is latent rather than live\n\nIt is safe TODAY only because the differ that fills the mutation is capability-gated upstream, so a\nlinks-less transport never produces a link mutation that reaches `_update_one_dispatch_links`. That\nis a real invariant — but **nothing local asserts it**, and nothing fails if it is broken. It is\nexactly the \"absence of errors is not evidence of function\" shape this epic has hit repeatedly.\n\n## Why the failure would be silent, which is the actual severity argument\n\nAn `AttributeError` at any of these sites lands inside a broad `except Exception` on the dispatch\npath. So a transport missing an opt-in capability would not crash the pass and would not surface a\nbridge alert — the mutation would simply never be applied, and the reconciler would report success.\nThat is the same silent-soft-failure shape as `d067-5d2f-bcee-46ae` (outbound status swallowed) and\nas the seven transport members that \"raised nothing while nothing worked\" during J9.\n\nThis matters more now than before Data Center existed: the whole point of the capability protocols\nis that a SECOND backend may legitimately not implement them, so the \"only one transport, and it has\neverything\" assumption that made this safe is exactly the assumption DC removes.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] each of the four capability call sites either checks the capability at runtime before calling\n      (the `hasattr` shape `fetcher.py:553`/`:590` already uses) or is proven unreachable for a\n      non-capable transport by an assertion that FAILS if the upstream gating is removed\n- [ ] a transport WITHOUT `add_comment` and WITHOUT `set_relationship` driven through the dispatch\n      path does not silently skip work: it either never reaches the site, or it produces an\n      observable bridge alert — asserted by a test, not by inspection\n- [ ] the chosen mechanism is consistent with `fetcher.py`'s, or the divergence is justified in a\n      comment\n- [ ] [operator-attested] mutation-checked: removing the guard (or the upstream gating the proof\n      relies on) turns the new test RED with a message naming the missing capability, not an\n      incidental `AttributeError`, with the verdict recorded as a comment here\n- [ ] `cast(...)` is NOT removed in favour of `Any` anywhere — the static enforcement cc77 added\n      must survive this change","id":"a3fa-e8d4-f4aa-4b51","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Capability call sites are cast but not runtime-guarded"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785704829588736001,"uuid":"47ff449f-4b28-45a2-877d-8ddf422f4e88"}